1
00:00:05,271 --> 00:00:08,027
On the morning of 5 August 1962,

2
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:11,195
Marilyn Monroe was found dead
at her home in Los Angeles.

3
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:12,519
She was 36.

4
00:00:14,451 --> 00:00:17,912
Within three days, the world had
read the first newspaper reports,

5
00:00:18,264 --> 00:00:19,759
and her funeral had been held.

6
00:00:21,511 --> 00:00:24,049
This is her story, told
through her own voice,

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:27,099
the films she made, and the
record of the days that followed.

8
00:00:33,851 --> 00:00:38,131
The Associated Press introduced
her in 1962 as Norma Jean Baker,

9
00:00:38,442 --> 00:00:41,739
the frightened waif who became the
movies' famous Marilyn Monroe.

10
00:00:43,450 --> 00:00:46,344
In a Life magazine interview,
published just before she died,

11
00:00:46,635 --> 00:00:49,108
she remembered a foster family
sending her to the movies,

12
00:00:49,259 --> 00:00:50,310
to get her out of the house.

13
00:00:50,861 --> 00:00:52,493
She sat up front, she said,

14
00:00:52,924 --> 00:00:57,139
"a little kid all alone." And,
in her words, "I loved it."

15
00:00:58,931 --> 00:01:02,389
Darryl Zanuck of Twentieth
Century-Fox saw a screen test of her,

16
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:04,734
after two other studios
had turned her down.

17
00:01:05,326 --> 00:01:06,759
He signed her to a contract.

18
00:01:08,451 --> 00:01:10,486
Her first small film parts followed.

19
00:01:10,917 --> 00:01:15,579
In 1949, she appeared beside Groucho
Marx, in the comedy Love Happy.

20
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:19,243
In November 1952,

21
00:01:19,354 --> 00:01:23,080
she was a guest on the radio show
of the ventriloquist Edgar Bergen,

22
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,754
in a comic sketch with his
dummy, Charlie McCarthy.

23
00:01:26,305 --> 00:01:29,179
The lines were scripted,
but the voice is hers.

24
00:01:41,471 --> 00:01:46,151
On 13 September 1953, she appeared
on The Jack Benny Program.

25
00:01:46,703 --> 00:01:49,519
Here is her own voice,
from that broadcast.

26
00:02:18,981 --> 00:02:21,457
On 14 January 1954,

27
00:02:21,547 --> 00:02:26,289
she married the baseball star
Joe DiMaggio, in San Francisco.

28
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:31,976
A month later, in February
1954, she flew to Korea.

29
00:02:32,407 --> 00:02:35,151
The United States Marine
Corps estimated that she sang

30
00:02:35,171 --> 00:02:37,725
for thirteen thousand men
of the First Marine Division.

31
00:02:38,316 --> 00:02:41,050
An Army film shows her
arriving by helicopter.

32
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:51,276
The marriage lasted nine months.

33
00:02:51,828 --> 00:02:53,705
It ended with a crowded
press conference,

34
00:02:53,755 --> 00:02:55,129
where her lawyer spoke for her.

35
00:03:01,701 --> 00:03:05,820
In 1953, three of her films
reached theaters: Niagara,

36
00:03:06,091 --> 00:03:09,349
Gentlemen Prefer Blondes,
and How to Marry a Millionaire.

37
00:03:11,181 --> 00:03:15,141
In Gentlemen Prefer Blondes, she sang
Diamonds Are a Girl's Best Friend,

38
00:03:15,312 --> 00:03:18,269
in a pink dress, surrounded
by men in black suits.

39
00:03:19,921 --> 00:03:25,961
Then came River of No Return, in 1954,
and The Seven Year Itch, in 1955.

40
00:03:26,692 --> 00:03:30,669
That film gave her the most famous
image of her career: the white dress.

41
00:03:32,421 --> 00:03:36,942
In New York, she began attending the
Actors Studio, led by Lee Strasberg.

42
00:03:37,614 --> 00:03:39,790
Seven years later, he
would give her eulogy.

43
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:47,440
The comedies came next: Bus Stop
in 1956, and in 1959, Some Like It Hot,

44
00:03:47,751 --> 00:03:50,015
which the American Film
Institute ranked first

45
00:03:50,035 --> 00:03:52,929
on its 2000 list of the
funniest American movies.

46
00:04:06,721 --> 00:04:10,939
Her last completed film, The
Misfits, opened in early 1961.

47
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:13,935
It was also the last film of her co-star,

48
00:04:13,955 --> 00:04:16,329
Clark Gable, who died
before it was released.

49
00:04:18,041 --> 00:04:19,754
In April 1962,

50
00:04:20,065 --> 00:04:22,090
she returned to
Twentieth Century-Fox

51
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:23,583
to make Something's Got to Give.

52
00:04:24,055 --> 00:04:26,189
Here is a costume
test from that month.

53
00:04:27,881 --> 00:04:33,020
Her best-known song came on 19
May 1962, at Madison Square Garden.

54
00:04:33,512 --> 00:04:35,395
Less than three months
before she died,

55
00:04:35,415 --> 00:04:37,810
she sang Happy Birthday
to President Kennedy.

56
00:05:11,721 --> 00:05:14,735
One witness from her last
months is still on record.

57
00:05:15,326 --> 00:05:17,420
The photographer Lawrence
Schiller was with her

58
00:05:17,470 --> 00:05:19,062
on the set of her final film.

59
00:05:19,653 --> 00:05:23,429
In 2012, he told Fox News
about the day she turned 36.

60
00:05:30,511 --> 00:05:31,623
On 9 June,

61
00:05:31,934 --> 00:05:35,070
the New York Times reported that
the studio had dismissed her

62
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:38,026
and filed a suit for five
hundred thousand dollars.

63
00:05:38,046 --> 00:05:39,359
The film was abandoned.

64
00:05:41,152 --> 00:05:43,498
Two months after that
birthday, she was dead.

65
00:05:50,191 --> 00:05:52,887
Saturday, 4 August 1962.

66
00:05:53,378 --> 00:05:56,374
What is known about that day comes
from press reports at the time,

67
00:05:56,485 --> 00:05:58,579
and those reports did
not always agree.

68
00:06:00,250 --> 00:06:01,432
Her psychiatrist, Dr.

69
00:06:01,452 --> 00:06:05,048
Ralph Greenson, said she called
him at 5:15 in the afternoon,

70
00:06:05,359 --> 00:06:07,092
upset and looking for reassurance.

71
00:06:07,362 --> 00:06:08,394
They talked for an hour.

72
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:10,658
He told her to take a drive to relax.

73
00:06:11,008 --> 00:06:11,679
She did not.

74
00:06:13,371 --> 00:06:17,113
Her housekeeper, Eunice Murray, said
that she went to her room at eight,

75
00:06:17,304 --> 00:06:18,798
and seemed to be in good spirits.

76
00:06:20,671 --> 00:06:22,586
Early on Sunday, 5 August,

77
00:06:22,757 --> 00:06:26,639
she was found dead at her home in
Brentwood, a district of Los Angeles.

78
00:06:28,291 --> 00:06:32,250
By Monday, the news was on the front
page of the Washington Evening Star:

79
00:06:32,622 --> 00:06:35,519
Death of Marilyn Monroe
Ends Turbulent Career.

80
00:06:37,371 --> 00:06:40,289
A coroner's special "suicide
team" was already at work.

81
00:06:40,801 --> 00:06:43,839
The paper said that the
evidence so far was conflicting.

82
00:06:45,570 --> 00:06:47,323
On Wednesday, 8 August,

83
00:06:47,714 --> 00:06:51,230
about fifteen people were invited
to her funeral service in Westwood.

84
00:06:51,721 --> 00:06:53,994
Police prepared for thousands of fans.

85
00:06:54,806 --> 00:06:57,340
By midnight, a hundred
waited outside.

86
00:06:58,910 --> 00:07:02,165
The eulogy was given by her
drama coach, Lee Strasberg.

87
00:07:02,556 --> 00:07:06,832
The typed text says: "Marilyn
Monroe was a legend." And then:

88
00:07:07,203 --> 00:07:08,935
"I did not know this Marilyn Monroe.

89
00:07:09,406 --> 00:07:12,120
We, gathered here today,
knew only Marilyn."

90
00:07:13,711 --> 00:07:15,605
Her belongings went into storage.

91
00:07:16,056 --> 00:07:18,933
Among them was a white piano
that her mother had bought,

92
00:07:19,244 --> 00:07:21,479
which she had kept
through all her adult years.

93
00:07:23,231 --> 00:07:27,322
On 17 August, the Los Angeles
coroner, Theodore Curphey,

94
00:07:27,342 --> 00:07:30,079
announced that he considered
her death a probable suicide.

95
00:07:31,770 --> 00:07:32,933
In 1982,

96
00:07:32,953 --> 00:07:36,269
the Los Angeles County District
Attorney reviewed the case again.

97
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:39,896
The review found no evidence
of murder or conspiracy,

98
00:07:39,986 --> 00:07:42,080
and said the original
finding still stood.

99
00:07:50,771 --> 00:07:54,472
The first newspapers could only say
that the evidence was conflicting.

100
00:07:54,804 --> 00:07:56,639
Decades of theories came after.

101
00:07:58,231 --> 00:08:00,331
Which Marilyn do you
remember: the legend,

102
00:08:00,462 --> 00:08:01,919
or the person her friends knew?
